---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "Asset Allocation — The Decision That Matters Most";
const description =
  "Why the split between asset classes drives outcomes more than security " +
  "selection, how to set one by goal and horizon, and why rebalancing works.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Investing / Asset allocation">
  <h1>Asset allocation</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Asset allocation is how you divide money between equity, debt, gold, cash and
    anything else. It gets a fraction of the attention that stock selection does,
    and it drives far more of the outcome.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it dominates</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Picking a better stock inside your equity allocation changes part of a part.
    Changing the equity allocation itself — from 30% to 70% — changes the
    character of the entire portfolio: its expected return, its worst year, and
    how it behaves in the year you actually need the money. The second decision is
    far larger, and it is the one most people never explicitly make.
  </p>

  <h2>Setting one</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>start_from_goals</code><span class="meta">not from a formula</span></div>
      <p>Each goal has a date. A house deposit in three years and retirement in
        thirty are different problems deserving different allocations. Allocating
        per goal rather than as one blended pot prevents the common error of
        holding near-term money in equity.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>emergency_fund_first</code><span class="meta">before any allocation</span></div>
      <p>Six months of expenses somewhere immediately accessible. Its job is to
        stop you selling long-term investments at the worst possible moment —
        which is exactly when emergencies tend to arrive.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>rules_of_thumb_are_starting_points</code><span class="meta">not answers</span></div>
      <p>"100 minus your age in equity" is a conversation starter. It knows
        nothing about your job security, your other assets, your dependants or
        your actual goals.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Rebalancing</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Over time the winners grow into a larger share than you chose. A 60/40 split
    after a strong equity run might be 75/25 — carrying materially more risk than
    you decided on, without you having decided anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rebalancing sells some of what rose and buys what did not, returning to the
    target. It feels wrong every single time, which is precisely why it works: it
    enforces selling high and buying low as a mechanical rule rather than a
    judgement made under pressure.
  </p>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>annually_or_on_drift</code><span class="meta">either works</span></div>
      <p>Once a year, or whenever an allocation drifts more than about five
        percentage points from target. More frequent rebalancing adds costs and
        tax without improving the result much.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>use_new_money_first</code><span class="meta">cheaper</span></div>
      <p>Directing fresh contributions to the underweight asset rebalances without
        selling, which avoids realising gains and paying tax on them.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Diversification, honestly</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Holding thirty stocks in one market is less diversified than it looks — in a
    decline, correlations rise and most things fall together. Genuine
    diversification comes from assets driven by different forces, which is what
    debt and gold contribute.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    It also means they will always be disappointing during a bull market. That is
    not a failure of the allocation; it is the cost of the insurance, paid in
    advance. An allocation you abandon in the third good year was never really an
    allocation.
  </p>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>

  <p class="lede">
    Next: <a href="/learn/equity-vs-debt/">equity vs debt</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/compounding/">compounding</a> ·
    <a href="/mutual-funds/">mutual funds</a>
  </p>
</Base>
